  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure has brought a pleasant start to the weekend. Despite \nsome scattered cloud cover, there is plenty of sunshine to go \naround. A couple models try to develop spotty showers this afternoon \nwith a shortwave passing through New England, but confidence was too \nlow to add PoPs as drier air will likely be too much to overcome. \nSoutherly flow and increasing cloud cover will help keep \ntemperatures slightly more mild overnight. Low temperatures will be \nin the 50s and low 60s. \n\nA weak low pressure system will approach the area early Sunday \nmorning. Scattered showers develop ahead of this system by \nmidday for portions of northeast PA and the Southern Tier. \nShower chances will increase across the area during the \nafternoon, though coverage will be scattered, especially across \ncentral NY. With cloud cover quick to spread northward, \ninstability will be limited. Still, isolated non-severe \nthunderstorms will be possible. Mixing of stronger flow aloft \nwill lead to gusts of 20 to 25 mph during the afternoon and \nevening. Showers will continue through the overnight hours and \ninto Monday morning as low-level moisture lingers behind the \ndeparting system. Peak PWAT values will be around 2 inches and \nwarm cloud depths will exceed 10k feet. Most of model guidance \nkeep the bulk of the precipitation south of the region as \nrainfall accumulations continue to trend down slightly. The best\npotential for heavy rainfall with localized totals of 1 inch or\nmore is across the Wyoming Valley and into the Poconos. \nFortunately, these areas have not seen as much rainfall recently\ncompared to other areas in the region and flash flood guidance \nis high. While there may be some ponding and localized urban \nissues, confidence is low in widespread flash flooding so no \nwatch was issued.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure returns on Tuesday and will stick around through early \nWednesday. A cold front is quick to move through late Wednesday into \nThursday morning with the next round of showers. While instability \nwill be limited, afternoon thunderstorms cannot be ruled out.\nThere is still some uncertainty on how quick these showers exit \nas high pressure builds back in on Thursday. Following the \nfront, cooler air fills in and temperatures will be below \naverage the rest of the week. High pressure will remain in \ncontrol through at least the start of next weekend with most of \nthe region expected to see a few days without rain. Some model guidance\nhave a weak low that moves up the coast late in the week. If \nthis system tracks close to the coastline, then there will be \npotential for showers to clip parts of the area.",
